# host-text-joins-in-nfc: pins that text from the Host arrives as NFC and
# that joining two Host texts normalises the seam (chapter 3, Text; ADR
# 0030). The Request's second argument is a lone U+0301, which is already
# NFC and one Character, so it is written literally. Joined after "e", it
# composes to U+00E9, one Character, which is the Request's result. A Host
# text that isn't NFC can't be written here, since the Core writes the
# Request back as the NFC value it built, so the Value Encoding case
# `text-model/host-text-normalised-to-nfc` pins the constructor normalising
# one. Its Fuel, allocation and state figures are Cost Model 0's, as the TS
# Core blessed them.

> load joiner identity=6e80b62c605727e9a5f8d8bf11abdaaee345fb29334e4fbba4e6c57a96b3cba5
> request d1 to=joiner message=join args=["e", "́"]
> pump clock=2026-09-30T09:00:00Z
seg joiner/r1 start delivery=d1 handler=join clause=1 fuel=28 alloc=50 state=32 end=return
run joiner/r1 outcome=completed delivery=d1 handler=join value="é" fuel=28 alloc=50
pumped state=idle fuel=28

> vars
vars joiner markLength=1 joinedLength=1
